{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1122328-social-penalities-for-losing-humanity","id":1122328,"title":"Social penalities for losing Humanity","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-07-29T07:11:45-07:00","id":1122328,"title":"Social penalities for losing Humanity","content":"Hi folks,<br /> <br /> I am really not liking that penalities. It reads you get the penalities when &quot;familiarize&quot; with humans. How would that work, I mean practically in game. To give the penality in all mortal interactions seems way too punishing for Vampires who are build for social interactions. Even considering that vampires can in my mind be really convincing and seductive even with low humanity. When it only means you can not &quot;get&quot; humans, it comes up not often enough and would be an &quot;Empathy&quot; penality in a nutshell and a bit boring. Additionally, even psychopath are fully able to analyze you in full, even if they don\u00b4t familiarize with you. They don\u00b4t &quot;get&quot; the feelings, but they understand what makes you tick.<br /> <br /> So how about this:<br /> <br /> You roll humanity in the following situations:<br /> <br /> a) When a mortal suspects you not to be human. If you fail, he knows...<br /> b) You roll if a mortal pleas for your compassion, maybe if he asks you to help him or if he is in grave danger. If you fail, you are unable to care.<br /> <br /> Would that not be better?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13653,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13653-narr666","user":"Narr666","date":".  typo 07-29-2017, 10:22 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13653-narr666","name":"Narr666","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"57"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-29T12:27:09-07:00","id":1122422,"title":"","content":"I think it's more empathy and associating with humans in a more humanly manner. So it'd be a penalty to socialize rolls, but not subterfuge rolls. A penalty to empathy rolls, but not intimidate rolls. Stuff like that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12436-ever-professional","name":"Ever Professional","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12436_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-29T13:39:42-07:00","id":1122463,"title":"","content":"The specific phrasing in 2e is &quot;relate.&quot; This isn't a blanket penalty to all social rolls for human interactions. It's a penalty on rolls that involve putting yourself in the other person's shoes. Vampires with low Humanity are going to have trouble with Soft Leverage (since you need to relate to the target to know how to appeal to them), but be just fine with Hard Leverage (where you can just use the instinct of self preservation against a target by threatening them with violence).<br /> <br /> There's no penalty to Discipline rolls there, and these penalties are (until Humanity 1), easily countered by using blood to thrall individuals.<br /> <br /> So socially built vampires can do just fine. The struggle is trying to be nice to mortals without also keeping your Humanity up. Being a cold manipulative bastard? Humanity isn't going to get in your way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-29T14:26:04-07:00","id":1122485,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1122328-social-penalities-for-losing-humanity?p=1122463#post1122463\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The specific phrasing in 2e is &quot;relate.&quot; This isn't a blanket penalty to all social rolls for human interactions. It's a penalty on rolls that involve putting yourself in the other person's shoes. Vampires with low Humanity are going to have trouble with Soft Leverage (since you need to relate to the target to know how to appeal to them), but be just fine with Hard Leverage (where you can just use the instinct of self preservation against a target by threatening them with violence).<br /> <br /> There's no penalty to Discipline rolls there, and these penalties are (until Humanity 1), easily countered by using blood to thrall individuals.<br /> <br /> So socially built vampires can do just fine. The struggle is trying to be nice to mortals without also keeping your Humanity up. Being a cold manipulative bastard? Humanity isn't going to get in your way.</div> </div> </div> </div>I am not convinnced. As I said, even Psychopaths can be really good at soft leverage. But it was interesting to hear your thoughts. I think I give my version a try, maybe it works.<br /> <br /> Thanks for the feedback.<br /> <br /> Dennis","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13653-narr666","name":"Narr666","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"57"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-29T14:59:56-07:00","id":1122503,"title":"","content":"I'm not really sure where psychopaths figure into this. Being a vampire isn't a personality disorder -- it's an unusual series of life experiences that induce profound alienation. The problems a low-Humanity vampire has relating to mortals (and &quot;relate&quot; is, indeed, a key term) have to do with the huge gulf in experience between an average person and one of the living dead. Don't think &quot;psychopath&quot; or &quot;sociopath&quot; or any of those kinds of terms.<br /> <br /> Instead, think about sitting down in a Starbucks with a kid you knew in high school... somebody who made the opposite of every choice you ever made. Somebody who got all the chances you didn't. Somebody whose daily routine is 100% composed of things you probably don't even do once a month. And then that guy wants advice on how to bridge the gulf with a family member you don't even have. And you just sit there, drawing a blank, because you cannot <i>imagine</i> what his life is like anymore.<br /> <br /> That's low Humanity.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:11px\">(EDIT: Also, in this metaphor, you don't like anything on the menu at Starbucks.)</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Cavaliers of Mars Creator</b><br /> <b>Retired CofD Lead</b><br /> <br /> Check out my <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Rose%20Bailey\" target=\"_blank\">guides to </a><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/261142/BITE-ME-How-to-Write-Vampire?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> and my <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse/pub/4895/Fantasy-Heartbreaker?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">indie games</a>!","edited":{"userid":2,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2-rose-bailey","user":"Rose Bailey","date":". 07-29-2017, 06:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":17}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2-rose-bailey","name":"Rose Bailey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2361"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-29T20:00:56-07:00","id":1122577,"title":"","content":"Even if you wanted to keep the psychopath comparison... a personal with a neurological condition that reduces their ability to empathize with the emotional states of other human beings and has to &quot;fake&quot; it to maintain positive social bonds? They have to do what vampires do to maintain Humanity. They have to stay involved with neutrotypical people to intellectually grasp what their brains doesn't process. A psychopath that takes the time to convincingly fake empathy, is equivalent to a vampire that maintains higher levels of Humanity. Overcoming such neurological conditions takes conscious effort and work.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-30T06:58:37-07:00","id":1122691,"title":"","content":"Before we all concentrate on the Psychopath comparison, I said that <b><u>even</u> </b>a Psychopath can do it, not that a vampire <b><u>is</u></b> a psychopath. But my interest more revolved around talking about an alternative rule to the social penality rather than talking about what low humanity is. A version how I like to play out low humanity can be found here: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1114615-%E2%80%8Bnarr%C2%B4s-thought-and-rules-revisions-requiem-2nd-edition-humanity\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/m...ition-humanity</a>.<br /> <br /> But how I see humanity is not really the point. I search more for a alternative that is clearer and more fun than the penalities, where you in my opinion have to think every time it is &quot;relating&quot; to a human or not and feel a bit boring. <br /> <br /> To avoid misunderstandings: I thought about a rule that would use the text as written, that makes it more difficult to relate to humans, without giving a simple penality to social roles.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13653-narr666","name":"Narr666","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"57"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-30T15:23:17-07:00","id":1122819,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Narr666</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1122328-social-penalities-for-losing-humanity?p=1122691#post1122691\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But how I see humanity is not really the point.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure... but that means you have to put your views on it aside and accept the RAW Humanity if we're just going to focus on if the RAW penalties are good/house rules are better aspect of things.<br /> <br /> Right now if feels like you're &quot;not convinced&quot; more because of how you see Humanity, not because of any points people have made.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I search more for a alternative that is clearer and more fun than the penalities, where you in my opinion have to think every time it is &quot;relating&quot; to a human or not and feel a bit boring. </div> </div> </div>OK, so focusing back on this:<br /> <br /> 1) Humanity is now your &quot;don't break the masquerade pool.&quot; this house rule creates a lot of mental hoops to jump through as a Humanity 10, Manipulation 1, Subterfuge 0 character is far better at covering up the signs that they're a vampire than a character with Humanity 5, Manipulation 5, and Subterfuge 5. I don't see how this generates fun at the table, as it divorces being convincing about not being a vampire completely from character proficiency at being convincing in general. It's also not an improvement on situational clarity, because you just replaced, &quot;is this a roll that involves relating to a human,&quot; with, &quot;is this a situation where a human suspects they're dealing with a supernatural.&quot; The ST has to constantly be deciding when in the ambiguous process of suspicion a human is suspicious enough to warrant a roll.<br /> <br /> 2) Rolling to care is decidedly boring and unfun. My character being unmoved for empathetic reasons doesn't mean they don't care. If my vampire thinks the human is a valuable pawn, he's going to rescue him because of that even if any appeals to morality or humanity were pointless to start with. Whether or not my character cares, and why they care, shouldn't be up to the dice. If my character as two Touchstones, both of which are also protected by the True Friend merit... why the heck should I ever have to roll to care about them? I built that character to explicitly care about them. It's not really engaging to play to have to roll to help them because they asked. That also brings in a lack of clarity. When I have to roll for this isn't clear. What's a plea for compassion vs. a plea for something else? <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-30T17:41:26-07:00","id":1122859,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Rose Bailey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1122328-social-penalities-for-losing-humanity?p=1122503#post1122503\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not really sure where psychopaths figure into this. Being a vampire isn't a personality disorder -- it's an unusual series of life experiences that induce profound alienation. The problems a low-Humanity vampire has relating to mortals (and &quot;relate&quot; is, indeed, a key term) have to do with the huge gulf in experience between an average person and one of the living dead. Don't think &quot;psychopath&quot; or &quot;sociopath&quot; or any of those kinds of terms.<br /> <br /> Instead, think about sitting down in a Starbucks with a kid you knew in high school... somebody who made the opposite of every choice you ever made. Somebody who got all the chances you didn't. Somebody whose daily routine is 100% composed of things you probably don't even do once a month. And then that guy wants advice on how to bridge the gulf with a family member you don't even have. And you just sit there, drawing a blank, because you cannot <i>imagine</i> what his life is like anymore.<br /> <br /> That's low Humanity.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:11px\">(EDIT: Also, in this metaphor, you don't like anything on the menu at Starbucks.)</span></div> </div> </div> </div>Save the guy who's asking for your advice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-31T17:42:44-07:00","id":1123250,"title":"","content":"The way I'm playing the term &quot;relate&quot; is as followed: imagine a human whose motivations are greed, ambition and envy. A Kindred would have no problem seeing through that but if he is acting out og genuine love or friendship or even concern, a Vampire might be puzzled by it. Unable to relate to such humane feelings he might not see these as valid and keep looking for the &quot;real&quot; motivation. In this case I would apply the Humanity's penalty. All in all I don't apply it very often.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1851-jps","name":"JPS","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1851_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"209"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-01T19:05:22-07:00","id":1123736,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JPS</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1122328-social-penalities-for-losing-humanity?p=1123250#post1123250\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The way I'm playing the term &quot;relate&quot; is as followed: imagine a human whose motivations are greed, ambition and envy. A Kindred would have no problem seeing through that but if he is acting out og genuine love or friendship or even concern, a Vampire might be puzzled by it. Unable to relate to such humane feelings he might not see these as valid and keep looking for the &quot;real&quot; motivation. In this case I would apply the Humanity's penalty. All in all I don't apply it very often.</div> </div> </div> </div>That really depends on the level of jadedness of the vampire. I can completely see a 500 years old elder understanding those motivations, but being himself unable to allow himself such feelings considering how fragile mortals are in comparison.<br /> <br /> It's like people who've been hurt by their crush, they stop being able to allow themselves to trust others with such a vulnerable part of their selves. But they can certainly understand where the person is coming from. It's defense mechanisms.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12191,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","user":"Malus","date":". 08-01-2017, 10:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-03T14:11:59-07:00","id":1124471,"title":"","content":"I have an unofficial rule or guide as a Storyteller. When I portray an NPC, I try to include two qualities, Role-play their dialogs and some action or mannerism description. But when vampire players are interacting with Humans NPC, I drop this qualities along with Humanity; first the descriptions and then the role-play dialog, i.e. I use my poker face. This encourage them to rely on dice to read the situation or the NPC, calling on the Humanity penalty more often (its a bit tricky with a party with a very diverse Humanity). I'll try an example: If a vamp player with Humanity 3 is questioning a Human, instead of me role-playing him slightly raising my voice and saying, &quot;I don't know anything!&quot; and add that he did so while avoiding to look at their face--- I just tell them in a calm voice, &quot;He says he doesn't know anything.&quot; A vamp with Humanity 4 would get the role-play dialog but no the description. Don't know if this helps in anyway.<br /> <br /> Additionally, Awe, first dot of the Majesty Discipline, voids a lot of penalties which is compatible with low Humanity vampires easily relating to Humans, so socially build vamps have no problems.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12173-basilistik","name":"Basilistik","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"33"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-04T09:18:11-07:00","id":1124804,"title":"","content":"Oh a curious issue? <br /> <br /> how is speaking with a well spoken charismatic low humanity vamp, likely to be different from a socially inept high humanity vamp, who both have the same adjusted dice pool other then the 1st is much more memorable.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-04T09:41:39-07:00","id":1124810,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Basilistik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1122328-social-penalities-for-losing-humanity?p=1124471#post1124471\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have an unofficial rule or guide as a Storyteller. When I portray an NPC, I try to include two qualities, Role-play their dialogs and some action or mannerism description. But when vampire players are interacting with Humans NPC, I drop this qualities along with Humanity; first the descriptions and then the role-play dialog, i.e. I use my poker face. This encourage them to rely on dice to read the situation or the NPC, calling on the Humanity penalty more often (its a bit tricky with a party with a very diverse Humanity). I'll try an example: If a vamp player with Humanity 3 is questioning a Human, instead of me role-playing him slightly raising my voice and saying, &quot;I don't know anything!&quot; and add that he did so while avoiding to look at their face--- I just tell them in a calm voice, &quot;He says he doesn't know anything.&quot; A vamp with Humanity 4 would get the role-play dialog but no the description. Don't know if this helps in anyway.<br /> <br /> Additionally, Awe, first dot of the Majesty Discipline, voids a lot of penalties which is compatible with low Humanity vampires easily relating to Humans, so socially build vamps have no problems.</div> </div> </div> </div>The only way I can see this working with any amount of verisimilitude is if the vampire in question has some intellectual disability or social developmental impairment.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-04T09:47:31-07:00","id":1124813,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prince of the Night</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1122328-social-penalities-for-losing-humanity?p=1124804#post1124804\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh a curious issue?<br /> <br /> how is speaking with a well spoken charismatic low humanity vamp, likely to be different from a socially inept high humanity vamp, who both have the same adjusted dice pool other then the 1st is much more memorable.</div> </div> </div> </div>They should be portrayed quite differently in role-play. The low-Humanity eloquent vampire's speech might seem refined, but with a very unsettling undercurrent to their words and body language - in turns disinterested, predatory, and perhaps confused or annoyed - when they try to communicate with mortals on a mortal level; on the other hand, they should still seem charming to vampires and other supernatural beings, if in an unconventional or even downright alien manner from a mortal perspective. The high-Humanity awkward vampire... just seems like your average, awkward human being who doesn't know how to use their words very well, whether the other person in the conversation is mortal or otherwise. At least, that's about how I'd run it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I've got a bit of a bad habit of extensively editing my posts after the fact. Please try and bear with me...<br /> In case you're comfortable being more friendly and casual about things, my name's Seren, or Es/Es for short. They/Them or Fae/Faer, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3946-sconce","name":"Sconce","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"127"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-04T22:50:02-07:00","id":1125111,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1122328-social-penalities-for-losing-humanity?p=1124810#post1124810\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The only way I can see this working with any amount of verisimilitude is if the vampire in question has some intellectual disability or social developmental impairment.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know if it's a social development impairment, but low Humanity is a disability, one that has a heavy mechanical weight, that may be worst than any normal condition you could apply to a mental state to relate to humans, so I see it as very real. For this types of Vampires, humans are very alien and hard to relate creatures to them. How would you portray an alien and hard to understand creature to players, when the players characters shouldn't understand it?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12173-basilistik","name":"Basilistik","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"33"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-05T07:19:35-07:00","id":1125202,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Basilistik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1122328-social-penalities-for-losing-humanity?p=1125111#post1125111\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't know if it's a social development impairment, but low Humanity is a disability, one that has a heavy mechanical weight, that may be worst than any normal condition you could apply to a mental state to relate to humans, so I see it as very real. For this types of Vampires, humans are very alien and hard to relate creatures to them. How would you portray an alien and hard to understand creature to players, when the players characters shouldn't understand it?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think it's less &quot;humans are alien&quot; to this vampire and more &quot;these short lived buffoons don't matter, not even as snacks&quot;. You may understand that the human is in distress, but ultimately not even your beast is concerned with them to the point where they aren't even an appropriate food source. It's plain and simple misanthropy.<br /> <br /> Take Patrick Bateman:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">I have all the characteristics of a human being: blood, flesh, skin, hair; but not a single, clear, identifiable emotion, except for [Thirst]* and disgust. Something horrible is happening inside of me and I don't know why. My nightly blood-lust has overflown into my days. I feel lethal, on the verge of frenzy. I think my mask of sanity is about to slip.</span></span></span> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}